	"EngineVersion": "5.8.0",
	"FriendlyName": "SpawnBudget - Budgeted World Spawner with Distance Rings and Actor Pooling",
	"Description": "A world spawner that cannot spike the frame. Not \"spawn everything that is due\" - at most N spawns and M despawns per frame, and never more than a set number of milliseconds; whatever is left over waits in a queue sorted by importance and continues on the next frame from exactly where it stopped. Nothing is lost and nothing is spawned twice. What gets spawned is decided by three distance rings around the player - SpawnIn, KeepAlive and Despawn - with the gap between the first and the last being deliberate hysteresis, so a player walking back and forth over a ring boundary produces no flicker. What disappears goes back into a per-class pool instead of into garbage collection: a despawn hides the actor, takes it off the tick and parks it, a spawn takes it back out, resets it and switches it on. The counter that matters is on the statistics box - Pool hits against New allocations - and in the steady state the second number stands still. That is the product in one line: not that it spawns, but that it stops costing anything once it has. A single world population ceiling applies on top; above it requests are refused and counted rather than quietly dropped. Sorting is distance divided by the weight of the source, so the nearest and most important arrives first, and an actor inside the camera cone may count double. Sources scatter over a point, a circle, a box or a spline, optionally projected onto the navigation mesh or the ground, and a scatter point that would land outside the despawn ring is rejected rather than spawned and immediately thrown away. Every number it claims is drawn on a UCanvas statistics box that survives a Shipping build: population against ceiling, spawns and despawns in this frame, queue length, milliseconds per tick, pool hits against new allocations, refused requests and active sources. Console commands Spawn.Stats, Spawn.Budget, Spawn.Population, Spawn.Clear and Spawn.Stress. One runtime module, no UMG, no Niagara, no Chaos and no editor module. Built and verified on Win64; Mac and Linux are enabled in the plugin descriptor but were not built for this release.",
	"Category": "Gameplay",
